= Temperature
{wiki}

Temperature labels <thermal equilibrium>: two internally equilibrated bodies have the same <temperature> when contact permitting only <heat> exchange produces no net <heat transfer>. The <zeroth law of thermodynamics> makes the comparison transitive. If bodies A and B separately reach <thermal equilibrium> with the same <thermometer> without appreciably disturbing either body, they agree with each other. This is what lets a <gas thermometer> compare a metal block with <water>, despite their different <internal energies> and <heat capacities>. Insulation alone makes no such comparison: it can prevent <heat transfer> between bodies at different <temperatures>. <University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/1.1 Temperature and Thermal Equilibrium>[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, University Physics Volume 2, section 1.1: temperature and the zeroth law]{ref}

The <zeroth law of thermodynamics> establishes equality comparisons, but does not choose a numerical scale. <Thermodynamic temperature> supplies an absolute scale consistent with <Carnot's theorem> and <entropy>. For a <simple compressible system> at <thermodynamic equilibrium> with fixed particle number $N$ and composition,
$$
T=\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial S}\right)_{V,N},\qquad
\frac1T=\left(\frac{\partial S}{\partial U}\right)_{V,N}.
$$
Here $U$ is <internal energy>, $S$ is <entropy>, and $V$ is <volume (thermodynamics)>; the second equality assumes the derivative can be inverted. Temperature is a <thermodynamic conjugate variable> to <entropy> and belongs to the <intensive and extensive thermodynamic quantities> as an intensive quantity. Its <partial derivative> tells us how much <internal energy> must change per unit change of <entropy> under the stated constraints. More general systems require their other mechanical and material variables to be held fixed too. https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S1#S1.SS2.SSS2[David Tong, Statistical Physics, section 1.2.2: temperature from the entropy derivative]{ref}

For an infinitesimal quantity $\delta Q>0$ of <heat> transferred from A to B, with no <work> or matter exchange, the equilibrium <entropy> changes give
$$
dS_{\rm total}=\delta Q\left(\frac1{T_B}-\frac1{T_A}\right).
$$
For ordinary positive <temperatures>, spontaneous <heat transfer> from $T_A>T_B$ increases total <entropy>; <thermal equilibrium> has $T_A=T_B$. This comparison uses changes in <entropy>, rather than the total <internal energy> contained in either body. A small hot object can have less <internal energy> than a large cool one. See <entropy maximization under thermal contact> for the equilibrium criterion and <negative temperature> for the special case of bounded <energy> spectra.

Express absolute <thermodynamic temperature> $T$ in <kelvin>, and a Celsius reading $t_C$ in <degree Celsius>:
$$
\frac{t_C}{{}^\circ\mathrm C}=\frac{T}{\mathrm K}-273.15,\qquad
\frac{\Delta t_C}{{}^\circ\mathrm C}=\frac{\Delta T}{\mathrm K}.
$$
The offset is exact: $300\,\mathrm K$ corresponds to $26.85\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, and $400\,\mathrm K$ to $126.85\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. Their absolute <temperature> ratio is $4/3$; dividing their Celsius readings gives a different number. Thus <gas thermometer> pressure ratios and <Carnot's theorem> require <thermodynamic temperature>. The modern <SI> definition of the <kelvin> fixes the <Boltzmann constant> $k_B=1.380649\times10^{-23}\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$; a reference bath realizes or transfers the scale rather than defining that constant.<The International System of Units (SI), ninth edition, SI Brochure>[section 2.3.1: kelvin and Celsius temperature]{ref}

Temperature is not universally the <mean> <kinetic energy> of a particle. In a classical, nonrelativistic <ideal gas> at <thermal equilibrium>, the <equipartition theorem> gives the mean translational <kinetic energy> relative to the gas's bulk motion:
$$
\left\langle\frac12m|\mathbf v-\mathbf u|^2\right\rangle=\frac32k_BT.
$$
Here $m$ is particle <mass>, $\mathbf v$ its <velocity>, and $\mathbf u$ the mean flow <velocity>. In this regime, different particle <masses> at the same <temperature> have equal mean translational <kinetic energies> but different characteristic speeds. A moving gas can also carry bulk <kinetic energy> without being hotter in its rest frame. Internal molecular modes and <quantum statistics> require further care; the <equipartition theorem> has classical assumptions, whereas the equilibrium <entropy> definition applies more widely. https://davidtong.org/teaching/statistical-physics/statmechhtml/S2#S2.SS2.SSS1[Tong, Statistical Physics, section 2.2.1: classical equipartition and its limits]{ref}

= International Temperature Scale of 1990
{parent=Temperature}
{c}
{title2=$T_{90}$}

= ITS-90
{synonym}
{c}

The <International Temperature Scale of 1990> is a practical scale for reproducible <temperature> measurements. Its assigned reference points and interpolation procedures approximate <thermodynamic temperature>. A calibrated <standard platinum resistance thermometer> transfers this scale over specified intervals; it does not define the modern <kelvin>.<The International System of Units (SI), ninth edition, SI Brochure>[§2.3.1: practical temperature scales, ITS-90, and thermodynamic temperature]{ref}

= Thermometer
{parent=Temperature}
{wiki}

A thermometer measures <temperature> through a physical response whose relation to <temperature> is known. Examples include the length change from <thermal expansion>, a change in <electrical resistance>, and the <pressure> of a <gas thermometer>. If the response <function> $X=f(T)$ is an <injective function> over the working range, its measured value determines $T=f^{-1}(X)$ through the <inverse function>. <Thermometer calibration> establishes that relation for the particular instrument and measurement conditions. Two matching readings are meaningful only after calibration to a common scale and with the probes sampling comparable equilibrium states. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-2-thermometers-and-temperature-scales[Ling, Moebs and Sanny, University Physics Volume 2, section 1.2: physical responses used in thermometers]{ref}

For a contact thermometer, the sensing part must reach <thermal equilibrium> with the region being measured. That region may differ from the rest of the object: an immersed probe near a heater can read a local <temperature> while the surrounding <water> remains cooler. A stable reading can also be a <steady state> set by competing <heat transfer> paths to the sample and the room. Good contact, adequate immersion, mixing where appropriate, and checks for spatial differences help establish which <temperature> the reading represents. These are measurement controls inferred from the <thermal equilibrium> requirement, not a guarantee that every flat trace is equilibrium.

A thermometer also changes the sample it touches. Consider an original toy calculation with an initially uniform sample at $T_o=350\,\mathrm K$, a probe at $T_p=300\,\mathrm K$, and constant whole-body <heat capacities> $C_o=100\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$ and $C_p=1\,\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. Suppose together they form an <isolated system>, with no <work> or <phase transition> and negligible contact <energy>. The <first law of thermodynamics> and <thermal equilibrium> give
$$
T_f=\frac{C_oT_o+C_pT_p}{C_o+C_p}=349.50495\ldots\,\mathrm K,\qquad
T_f-T_o=\frac{C_p}{C_o+C_p}(T_p-T_o).
$$
The final <thermometer> reading underestimates the sample's original <temperature> by about $0.495\,\mathrm K$, although it correctly measures their new common <temperature>. The disturbance becomes small when the participating probe <heat capacity> is small compared with the sample's. A large controlled <heat bath> is a different limit: it supplies or removes <heat> while approximately maintaining its own <temperature>.

Waiting time is a separate issue from disturbance. Under a toy <Newton's law of cooling> model for a uniform probe in a maintained <heat bath>, a constant contact conductance $G$ gives
$$
C_p\frac{dT_p}{dt}=G(T_b-T_p),\qquad
T_p(t)=T_b+[T_p(0)-T_b]e^{-t/\tau},\qquad \tau=\frac{C_p}{G}.
$$
The <time constant> $\tau$ controls this <exponential decay>. For stipulated values $T_b=350\,\mathrm K$, $T_p(0)=300\,\mathrm K$ and $\tau=5\,\mathrm s$, the probe reads about $331.61\,\mathrm K$ after one <time constant> and $347.51\,\mathrm K$ after three. These are calculated values, not an instrument specification or measured data. Real probes may have multiple relaxation times and additional <heat transfer> paths; choose an equilibration criterion from the required accuracy, rather than assuming that a fixed waiting time works universally.

= Resistance thermometer
{parent=Thermometer}
{wiki}

A <thermometer> that infers <temperature> from a calibrated <electrical resistance>. Measuring current can heat its sensing element; extrapolation toward zero current controls that bias in the <saturation-pressure equilibrium measurement>.

= Platinum resistance thermometer
{parent=Resistance thermometer}

A <resistance thermometer> using <platinum> as its sensing material. It provides the <temperature> readout in the <saturation-pressure equilibrium measurement>.

Transduction turns <electrical resistance> into a <temperature> indication. A uniform wire has $R=\rho_{\rm el}\ell/A$, with <electrical resistivity> $\rho_{\rm el}$, <length> $\ell$, and cross-sectional <area> $A$. Stronger atomic vibrations at higher <temperature> scatter <electrons> more, increasing <platinum>'s <electrical resistivity>; impurities and <crystallographic defects> also contribute.<Guide on Secondary Thermometry: Industrial Platinum Resistance Thermometers by Jonathan Pearce and others>[Guide on Secondary Thermometry: Industrial Platinum Resistance Thermometers, §2.1, printed pp. 6–7: resistivity, electron scattering, and impurities]{ref}

A small known <electric current> $I$ and measured <electric potential difference> $V$ give $R=V/I$ through <Ohm's law>; a <resistance bridge> can instead compare the element with a reference <resistor>. Inverting the calibrated response then gives the sensing element's <temperature>. This makes electrical readout convenient, but the <platinum resistance thermometer lead resistance error>, <resistance thermometer self-heating>, and <resistance thermometer immersion test> explain why a stable display alone is insufficient. The <gas thermometer> supplies a contrasting route through an <equation of state>; the <saturation-pressure equilibrium measurement> shows why a trustworthy independent <temperature> readout matters.

= Standard platinum resistance thermometer
{parent=Platinum resistance thermometer}

= SPRT
{synonym}
{c}

A <standard platinum resistance thermometer> uses very pure, annealed <platinum> with strain-limiting construction. An <industrial platinum resistance thermometer> commonly uses supported wire or a film. Their response curves differ.<Guide on Secondary Thermometry: Industrial Platinum Resistance Thermometers by Jonathan Pearce and others>[§§1–2.2, printed pp. 5–11: industrial elements versus SPRTs and wire/film construction]{ref}

The word standard describes the instrument's metrological role. Calibrated <SPRTs> realize specified interpolation intervals of the <International Temperature Scale of 1990> and transfer reference <temperatures> to other <thermometers>.<Mercury Thermometer Alternatives: Platinum Resistance Thermometers (PRTs) by National Institute of Standards and Technology>[Platinum Resistance Thermometers, opening explanation: SPRTs as ITS-90 interpolation tools]{ref} They are primary standards for realizing that practical scale, but their <electrical resistance> alone does not determine absolute <thermodynamic temperature> without calibration. Primary thermometry such as the corrected <gas thermometer> obtains <thermodynamic temperature> from a physical law. A reference in a comparison bath may be an <SPRT>; an individually calibrated <industrial platinum resistance thermometer> can also serve as a working reference with an appropriate uncertainty.

= Industrial platinum resistance thermometer
{parent=Platinum resistance thermometer}

= IPRT
{synonym}
{c}

An <industrial platinum resistance thermometer> is a <platinum resistance thermometer> built for practical measurement conditions. The label <Pt100> specifies a nominal reference <electrical resistance>, not an <SPRT>, a calibration certificate, or a universal accuracy. Calibrate the probe together with its readout when the desired result is the performance of the complete indicating system.

= Pt100
{parent=Industrial platinum resistance thermometer}
{c}
{title2=$R_0=100\,\Omega$}

A <Pt100> has nominal <electrical resistance> $100\,\Omega$ at $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$.<Mercury Thermometer Alternatives: Platinum Resistance Thermometers (PRTs) by National Institute of Standards and Technology>[Platinum Resistance Thermometers, opening explanation: conventional 100-ohm element at 0 °C]{ref} Here $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ is the reference for the nominal element designation; it is distinct from the $0.01\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ water <triple point> used in <SPRT> calibration. The following <Pt100 local linear model> deliberately stipulates one approximate response for calculations.

= Pt100 local linear model
{parent=Pt100}
{c}
{title2=$R=R_0(1+\alpha t)$}

Choose the illustrative local model
$$
R(t)=R_0(1+\alpha t),\qquad
R_0=100\,\Omega,\qquad
\alpha=0.00385\,({}^\circ\mathrm C)^{-1}.
$$
Here $t$ is the Celsius <temperature>, measured relative to $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, so $\alpha t$ is dimensionless. For this chosen <affine function>, the <derivative> and inverse relation are
$$
\frac{dR}{dt}=R_0\alpha=0.385\,\Omega/{}^\circ\mathrm C,\qquad
t=\frac{R/R_0-1}{\alpha},\qquad
\Delta t=\frac{\Delta R}{0.385\,\Omega/{}^\circ\mathrm C}.
$$
Calculated values are $R(0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C)=100\,\Omega$, $R(20\,{}^\circ\mathrm C)=107.70\,\Omega$, $R(25\,{}^\circ\mathrm C)=109.625\,\Omega$, and $R(50\,{}^\circ\mathrm C)=119.25\,\Omega$. These are model outputs, not measurements or an assertion that any real <Pt100> follows this line exactly. The number $0.00385$ here is the chosen slope parameter; it is not a universal local <derivative> for all <platinum resistance thermometers>. Curvature, manufacturing differences, and the fitted calibration interval matter. A wider-range calibration uses a validated response curve rather than extrapolating this line indefinitely.

\Image[platinum-resistance-temperature-calibration.png]
{id=platinum-resistance-temperature-calibration}
{title=Chosen Pt100 line and an illustrative calibration with an independent check}
{description=The left panel plots the <Pt100 local linear model>. The right panel uses stipulated values for a different illustrative element: two points set a line, while a third point tests it. Every point is synthetic; none is measured calibration data or a tolerance band.}
{source=platinum-resistance-temperature-calibration.py}

For a separate synthetic <thermometer calibration>, stipulate $R_a=100.20\,\Omega$ at $t_a=0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ and $R_b=119.60\,\Omega$ at $t_b=50\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. A two-point <affine function> has slope $0.388\,\Omega/{}^\circ\mathrm C$ and gives
$$
t=\frac{R-100.20\,\Omega}{0.388\,\Omega/{}^\circ\mathrm C}.
$$
Thus $R=109.90\,\Omega$ would indicate $25\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. If an additional synthetic check at reference $25\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ instead supplied $110.10\,\Omega$, the line would indicate $25.515\ldots\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. The $0.20\,\Omega$ residual exposes disagreement that two fitted points cannot reveal. This separate example changes both offset and slope; it is not a replacement specification for the nominal <Pt100 local linear model>.

= Platinum resistance thermometer comparison calibration
{parent=Platinum resistance thermometer}

A 1998 NIST account describes stirred comparison baths, a reference <SPRT> with a 30 Hz <resistance bridge>, and a temperature-controlled $100\,\Omega$ reference <resistor>. The test <IPRTs> used a $1\,\mathrm{mA}$ <current source>, a scanner, and an 8½-digit <voltmeter>. Separate current and voltage leads made each test probe a four-terminal device; readings in both current directions supplied voltage ratios to the reference <resistor>.<A New NIST Automated Calibration System for Industrial-Grade Platinum Resistance Thermometers by Gregory F. Strouse and others>[A New NIST Automated Calibration System for Industrial-Grade Platinum Resistance Thermometers, §§3.1, 3.3 and 4.2, printed pp. 2–6: baths, readout, four-terminal devices and reversed current]{ref}

Its program bracketed test readings with reference <SPRT> readings and repeated a block if bath drift was excessive. A check <SPRT> monitored the system; reference water-<triple point> measurements checked stability. Sensor depths were matched, and unfamiliar probes underwent immersion tests. Ice and water-<triple point> readings before and after calibration checked changes. Additional points could test the fitted curve. Reported calibration uncertainties excluded transport, subsequent drift, <hysteresis>, and the user's measurement system. These are controls and limits of that documented apparatus, not an accuracy promise for all <platinum resistance thermometers>.<A New NIST Automated Calibration System for Industrial-Grade Platinum Resistance Thermometers by Gregory F. Strouse and others>[§1, printed p. 2; §4.2, printed pp. 5–6; §5.4, printed p. 9: excluded effects, drift rejection, check thermometer, immersion and extra fit checks]{ref}

The inference is a pair: reference <temperature> from the <SPRT>, test <electrical resistance> from the electrical readout. Record measuring <electric current>, wiring, immersion, and the stated reference scale with each pair. A bath controller's setpoint is not itself the calibrated reference <temperature>. Returning to a reference and checking intermediate points distinguishes a fitted relation from a reproducible calibration over the intended interval.

= Platinum resistance thermometer lead resistance error
{parent=Platinum resistance thermometer}
{title2=$\Delta t=(r_1+r_2)/(R_0\alpha)$}

With two shared leads of <electrical resistances> $r_1,r_2$, <Ohm's law> gives
$$
V_{\rm two}=I[R(t)+r_1+r_2],\qquad
R_{\rm indicated}=R(t)+r_1+r_2.
$$
Using the <Pt100 local linear model>, positive lead <electrical resistance> appears as a warmer <temperature>. At true $25\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, stipulated leads of $0.50\,\Omega$ each change $109.625\,\Omega$ into $110.625\,\Omega$, producing $27.597\ldots\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$: a calculated bias of about $2.60\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. Subtracting a measured $1.00\,\Omega$ loop correction works only while that loop <electrical resistance> remains appropriate; cable <temperature> changes can invalidate it.

\Image[platinum-resistance-two-wire-four-wire-sensing.png]
{id=platinum-resistance-two-wire-four-wire-sensing}
{title=Two shared leads include cable voltage drops; four leads sense at the platinum element}
{description=Red paths carry <electric current>; blue paths connect an ideal high-input-<electrical resistance> <voltmeter>. In the two-wire circuit the sensing terminals span both leads and the element. In <four-terminal sensing> they span the element alone. The stated lead values and <temperature> bias are illustrative calculations.}
{source=platinum-resistance-two-wire-four-wire-sensing.py}

<Four-terminal sensing> separates the <electric current> and <electric potential difference> leads. Negligible sensing <electric current> suppresses sensing-lead drops; force-lead drops lie outside the interval. Real <voltmeter loading>, connections, and leakage still need checking.<Guide on Secondary Thermometry: Industrial Platinum Resistance Thermometers by Jonathan Pearce and others>[§§4.4.1 and 4.4.3, printed pp. 30 and 32–33: two-wire inclusion of leads and four-wire exclusion]{ref}

For a stable offset $E$ from the <thermoelectric effect>, two signed <electric potential differences> obey $V_+=IR+E$ and $V_-=-IR+E$. The current-reversal calculation in <four-terminal sensing> yields
$$
R=\frac{V_+-V_-}{2I}.
$$
At stipulated $I=1\,\mathrm{mA}$, $R=109.625\,\Omega$ and $E=3\,\mathrm{\mu V}$, $V_+=109.628\,\mathrm{mV}$ and $V_-=-109.622\,\mathrm{mV}$ recover $R$ exactly in this model. If the offset or sample <temperature> changes between readings, cancellation is incomplete. Reversal also leaves <Joule heating> unchanged because it depends on $I^2$.

= Resistance thermometer self-heating
{parent=Resistance thermometer}
{title2=$P=I^2R$}

Measuring <electric current> creates <Joule heating>, raising the sensing <temperature>. At fixed bath <temperature>, compare settled readings at $I$ and $\sqrt2I$ and extrapolate the approximately linear $I^2$ dependence toward zero current.<Guide on Secondary Thermometry: Industrial Platinum Resistance Thermometers by Jonathan Pearce and others>[§4.1, printed pp. 24–26, equation (4.1.1): self-heating, two-current extrapolation, and medium dependence]{ref}

Under the local model $R(I)=R_{\rm zero}+kI^2$,
$$
R_{\rm zero}=2R(I)-R(\sqrt2I).
$$
For synthetic readings $109.629\,\Omega$ at $1\,\mathrm{mA}$ and $109.633\,\Omega$ at $\sqrt2\,\mathrm{mA}$, the extrapolated value is $109.625\,\Omega$. The lower-current excess $0.004\,\Omega$ corresponds to $0.01039\ldots\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ in the <Pt100 local linear model>. At $109.625\,\Omega$, $1\,\mathrm{mA}$ dissipates $109.625\,\mathrm{\mu W}$; power alone does not specify a <temperature> rise without a thermal coupling model. These stipulated values are not measurements.

Repeat the comparison after settling and test the $I^2$ approximation if necessary. Changing from liquid to air changes <heat transfer>; a self-heating correction need not transfer unchanged. The <saturation-pressure equilibrium measurement> provides an existing example of controlling this disturbance in resistance thermometry.

= Resistance thermometer immersion test
{parent=Resistance thermometer}

A probe's sheath and leads carry <heat> between the sensing region and the surroundings through <thermal conduction>. In a stable, sufficiently uniform <heat bath>, compare the indicated <temperature> as insertion depth increases, allowing settling each time. A plateau supports adequate immersion for that apparatus and required uncertainty; it does not prove that every depth or medium works. Keep the sensing element and reference <thermometer> at comparable depths, and check bath gradients independently, so moving the probe does not merely sample a different <temperature>. The documented <platinum resistance thermometer comparison calibration> includes these controls.

= Gas thermometer
{parent=Thermometer}
{title2=$P\propto T\quad(N,V\text{ fixed})$}

A gas thermometer infers <temperature> from the <equation of state> of a confined <gas>. The constant-<volume (thermodynamics)> version measures <pressure>; the constant-<pressure> version measures <volume (thermodynamics)>. For an <ideal gas>,
$$
PV=Nk_BT=nRT,\qquad
\frac{T}{T_0}=\frac{P}{P_0}\quad(N,V\text{ fixed}),\qquad
\frac{T}{T_0}=\frac{V}{V_0}\quad(N,P\text{ fixed}).
$$
Here $N$ is particle number, $n$ is <amount of substance>, $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant>, and $R$ is the <molar gas constant>. The reference and test states must be internally equilibrated, and $T_0$ must be known on the absolute <thermodynamic temperature> scale. A pressure ratio cancels the unknown gas inventory and bulb size in the ideal constant-volume limit. This cancellation is the method's appeal: a mechanical measurement transfers an absolute <temperature> scale between different materials. The <Maxwell speed distribution> and <ideal gas pressure from wall collisions> explain the microscopic origin of the response. <University Physics Volume 2 by Samuel J. Ling and others/2.1 Molecular Model of an Ideal Gas>[OpenStax, University Physics Volume 2, section 2.1: the ideal gas law]{ref}

For an original ideal-model calculation, choose $T_0=300\,\mathrm K$, $P_0=120\,\mathrm{kPa}$ and $P=160\,\mathrm{kPa}$. Then $T=400\,\mathrm K$. These are stipulated values, not observations. Both <pressures> must be absolute, referred to vacuum. If the surrounding <atmospheric pressure> were $100\,\mathrm{kPa}$, the corresponding <gauge pressures> would be $20$ and $60\,\mathrm{kPa}$; their ratio is three, which would incorrectly predict $900\,\mathrm K$. A pressure difference from the atmosphere cannot be substituted into the <ideal gas law>.

\Image[temperature-thermometry-gas-apparatus.png]
{title=An original schematic of constant-volume <gas thermometer> measurements}
{description=Simplified measurement layout inspired by the NBS apparatus, not a replica or a report of measurements. A controlled bath thermalizes the bulb, separate calibrated <thermometers> check its surroundings, and a diaphragm transmits <pressure> to an absolute-pressure standard without mixing gases. The connecting tube contains gas at other <temperatures>; its inventory belongs in the <gas thermometer dead-space correction>.}

\b[A motivating calibration experiment.] Schooley's account of the NBS/NIST work describes a <helium>-filled metal bulb, a capillary, a <capacitance diaphragm gauge>, and a mercury pressure standard. Circulating bath liquid passed the bulb assembly and platinum resistance <thermometers>. The resistance probes checked the surrounding <temperature>; gas <pressure> supplied the independent thermodynamic comparison. In an early series, workers filled the cleaned bulb with purified <helium> at the highest test <temperature>, checked <pressure> over three hours, then measured at lower <temperatures> and near a reference at $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$. Six filling pressures allowed extrapolation toward zero pressure. https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/jres/095/jresv95n3p255_A1b.pdf[J. F. Schooley (1990), NBS/NIST Gas Thermometry From 0 to 660 °C, sections 4–5]{ref}

To turn this principle into a readable measurement protocol, use the following controls. These steps are a proposed teaching interpretation of the cited apparatus, rather than additional reported NBS observations:
* \b[Define the inventory.] Evacuate and clean the bulb and connecting tube, fill with a known pure <gas>, close the filling valve, and keep the same total particle number during each reference/test comparison. Monitor <pressure> at a maintained <temperature> before and after a run. A leak changes $N$; a slow drift can also reflect contamination, sorption, or imperfect equilibration, so pressure stability alone does not identify its cause.
* \b[Measure the reference.] Place the bulb in a controlled environment with independently assigned $T_0$. Record $P_0$ after thermalization, together with the independent <thermometer> readings and their spread. A reference near $0\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$ has an absolute <temperature> near $273.15\,\mathrm K$, rather than zero <temperature>. Repeating reference readings tests whether the inventory or apparatus changed during the comparison.
* \b[Measure the test state.] Change the bath setting, allow the bulb and <gas> to settle, and record $P$ only when the surrounding <thermometers> and <pressure> are sufficiently stable for the intended accuracy. Compare readings at different positions and vary the settling time. Record the connecting tube's <temperature> profile too: uniform bath <temperature> does not make the entire connecting tube isothermal.
* \b[Measure the volume changes.] Determine the bulb's <thermal expansion> and its deformation from differences between inside and outside <pressure>. Measure the connecting tube's dimensions and the effective valve volume. “Constant volume” is the ideal model; a real vessel expands. These measurements supply corrections, even though the absolute bulb volume cancels in the simplest pressure ratio.
* \b[Measure pressure against a reference.] For a liquid-column pressure standard, measure the vertical column height, the liquid's <temperature>-dependent <mass density>, local gravitational acceleration, and the reference-side <pressure>. Include diaphragm offsets and the gas's <hydrostatic pressure> change between the sensor and bulb. The pressure needed in the <equation of state> is at the bulb, not automatically at the remote gauge.

The corresponding NBS apparatus used a protective case with an outer <helium> layer held close to the bulb <pressure>, reducing pressure deformation while aiding thermal contact. <Thermal expansion> was measured on bulb material. Gas in the capillary remained partly outside the hot region, requiring a measured tube <temperature> profile and geometry. Pure working <gas> also mattered: contaminants that sorb on surfaces can change the freely circulating inventory. These are physical effects to measure or bound, rather than scatter that disappears by averaging. https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/IR/nbsir85-3133.pdf[J. F. Schooley, Thermometry, NBS IR 85-3133, chapter V, gas thermometry and the NBS experiment]{ref}

For illustration, neglect connecting-tube gas and describe a uniform bulb with a dilute <virial expansion> in particle <number density> $\rho_N=N/V$:
$$
PV=Nk_BT\left[1+B_2(T)\rho_N+O(\rho_N^2)\right],\qquad
\frac{T}{T_0}=\frac{P}{P_0}\frac{V(T)}{V(T_0)}\frac{Z(T_0)}{Z(T)}.
$$
Here $Z=PV/(Nk_BT)$ and $B_2$ is the <second virial coefficient> in the particle-density convention; a molar-density convention rescales the coefficient. The second relation follows exactly from the definition of $Z$ for the same $N$, and shows the signs of the corrections. If the bulb expands by $0.1\%$ and $Z=1$ at both states, the toy pressure ratio above gives $400.4\,\mathrm K$, rather than $400\,\mathrm K$. Improved expansion and <helium> nonideality models really changed the temperatures inferred from the 1989 NIST data: Harvey's 2023 reanalysis found expansion updates of roughly $1$–$3\,\mathrm{mK}$ and nonideality updates of roughly $-0.1$ to $-0.5\,\mathrm{mK}$. Those are reported corrections to historical results, not the much larger illustrative correction just calculated. https://tsapps.nist.gov/publication/get_pdf.cfm?pub_id=956523[Allan H. Harvey (2023), Small Corrections to 1989 NIST Constant-Volume Gas Thermometry Data, sections 2–3]{ref}

With equal corrected bulb <volumes>, negligible or corrected connecting-tube gas, fixed reference and test <temperatures>, and a dilute classical single gas phase,
$$
\frac{P}{P_0}=\frac{T}{T_0}\left[1+\bigl(B_2(T)-B_2(T_0)\bigr)\rho_N+O(\rho_N^2)\right],\qquad
\lim_{\rho_N\to0}\frac{P}{P_0}=\frac{T}{T_0}.
$$
Repeat the reference/test comparison for several sealed fills, reducing $N$ between comparisons but keeping it fixed within each pair. The extrapolation varies density at fixed endpoint <temperatures>; it does not cool one fill toward zero <temperature>. Real gases have different finite-density corrections but share the classical <ideal gas> intercept under these assumptions. The limit does not remove leaks, bulb expansion, gauge offsets, or the <gas thermometer dead-space correction>. At very low <pressures>, <thermal transpiration> can make remote sensor and bulb <pressures> differ through a nonisothermal capillary; this must be corrected too. The NBS work measured this pressure correction using a separate comparison of narrow and wider tubes. https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/jres/095/jresv95n3p255_A1b.pdf[Schooley (1990), sections 7.3–7.6: pressure, volume, dead space and gas nonideality]{ref}

\Image[temperature-thermometry-pressure-extrapolation.png]
{title=Ideal pressure ratios and a dilute-limit <gas thermometer> comparison in two toy gas models}
{description=Left: the stipulated ideal constant-volume calibration $P=0.4\,\mathrm{kPa\,K^{-1}}T$. Right: apparent <temperature> $T_{\rm app}=300\,\mathrm K\,P/P_0$ for invented dimensionless fill $x=\rho_N/\rho_*$, with $P/P_0=(4/3)(1+0.03x)/(1+0.01x)$ for model A and $(4/3)(1-0.02x)/(1+0.01x)$ for model B. Both approach the stipulated $400\,\mathrm K$ intercept; neither curve represents a named gas or measured data.}

At $x=1$, the toy models give $T_{\rm app}=407.92\,\mathrm K$ and $388.12\,\mathrm K$, respectively, although their imposed true test <temperature> is the same. Their zero-density intercept is the reusable quantity. Measuring several low-density points permits a fit and a check for curvature; asserting a straight line from one finite-density point supplies neither. A practical fit must balance nonideality against increasing relative pressure error and the capillary corrections, and keep its extrapolation uncertainty.

= Gas thermometer dead-space correction
{parent=Gas thermometer}

Gas outside the uniform-temperature bulb of a <gas thermometer> contributes to its conserved particle inventory. An idealized calculation divides the connecting tube into small regions with <volumes> $V_j$ and local <temperatures> $T_j$. Neglect <hydrostatic pressure> offsets and <thermal transpiration>, assume a common <pressure> $P$ and a local <ideal gas law> in each region, and let the bulb have <volume (thermodynamics)> $V_b$ and <temperature> $T_b$. Adding the particle inventories gives
$$
N=\frac{P}{k_B}\left(\frac{V_b}{T_b}+\sum_j\frac{V_j}{T_j}\right).
$$
Thus even an <ideal gas> in a perfectly rigid sealed apparatus need not obey $P\propto T_b$: some of its particles remain in regions with other <temperatures>. For a stipulated bulb of $100\,\mathrm{cm^3}$, a connecting volume of $1\,\mathrm{cm^3}$ maintained at $300\,\mathrm K$, and bulb reference and test <temperatures> of $300$ and $400\,\mathrm K$, conservation of $N$ gives
$$
\frac{P}{P_0}=\frac{100/300+1/300}{100/400+1/300}
=\frac{101}{76}\approx1.328947,\qquad
T_{\rm app}=300\,\mathrm K\frac{P}{P_0}\approx398.684\,\mathrm K.
$$
These are toy dimensions and a calculated bias, not measured data. Reducing $N$ reduces both <pressures> proportionally and leaves this ratio unchanged: extrapolating away gas nonideality does not eliminate the connecting-volume error. Actual apparatus also requires the tube's <thermal expansion>, a measured <temperature> profile, and the pressure corrections omitted in this model.

= Thermometer calibration
{parent=Thermometer}

<Thermometer> calibration compares an instrument's response with assigned reference <temperatures> under specified conditions. In a proposed two-reference procedure, thermalize the sensing part at $T_0$ and $T_1$, record responses $X_0$ and $X_1$, and, only if a linear response is justified over that interval, use
$$
T(X)=T_0+(T_1-T_0)\frac{X-X_0}{X_1-X_0}.
$$
Additional reference <temperatures> test curvature; returning to the first reference tests drift. A <gas thermometer> instead obtains its response from an <equation of state>, with a reference and corrections for its apparatus. In either case, uncertainty in the reference propagates to the inferred <temperature>. Calibration determines the response relation; it cannot by itself remove the finite-sample disturbance or incomplete <thermal equilibrium> described in <thermometer>. A reference established on a practical scale can also differ slightly from true <thermodynamic temperature>; the BIPM distinguishes the International Temperature Scale of 1990 readings from their thermodynamic counterparts.<The International System of Units (SI), ninth edition, SI Brochure>[section 2.3.1: practical and thermodynamic temperature]{ref}

= Kelvin
{parent=Temperature}
{title2=$\mathrm K$}

The kelvin is the SI unit of <thermodynamic temperature>, with symbol $\mathrm K$. Its definition fixes the numerical value of the <Boltzmann constant>; it is written without a degree sign.https://www.bipm.org/en/si-base-units/kelvin[BIPM, SI unit kelvin]{ref}

= Degree Celsius
{parent=Temperature}
{title2=${}^\circ\mathrm C$}

The degree Celsius has the same size as the <kelvin>, with a shifted temperature origin: $t/{}^\circ\mathrm C=T/\mathrm K-273.15$. Thus a change of one degree Celsius is a change of one <kelvin>, but temperature ratios in <Carnot's theorem> must use absolute <thermodynamic temperature>.<The International System of Units (SI), ninth edition, SI Brochure>{ref}

= Inverse temperature
{parent=Temperature}
{title2=$\beta=1/(k_BT)$}

<Inverse temperature> is $\beta=1/(k_BT)$, where $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant> and $T$ is the absolute <temperature>. A <canonical ensemble> weights an energy $E$ by its <Boltzmann factor> $e^{-\beta E}$. Calling couplings dimensionless means absorbing this factor $\beta$ into their energy coefficients. The alternative convention $k_B=1$ makes <inverse temperature> $1/T$; physical and dimensionless couplings must not be mixed.

In a thermal <Euclidean worldline path integral> with $\hbar=1$, $\beta$ is also the circumference of the imaginary-time circle.

